on december 7 2021 ubisoft announced its  first project in the nft space titled  ubisoft quartz and while this isn't the  first time nfts and games have crossed  paths it is the first time a triple-a  studio has entered the fray and it most  likely won't be the last so what might  nfts mean for the gaming industry going  forward and what the hell are these nft  things anyway in this video we're going  to talk about that and more however if  you already know what nfts are then feel  free to skip ahead to the more  gamer-centric stuff no hard feelings i  promise  for starters the letters nft stand for  non-fungible token but an easier way to  think about this is non-replaceable  token an example i've seen quite a lot  is the mona lisa it's unique and can't  be replaced by something like a mona  lisa poster whereas a dollar bill is  fungible i can replace it with another  dollar bill because they hold the same  value  well not when you combine them because  then that makes two dollars i'm talking  about if i lost one of them an nft can  be things like art music and even memes  but it's important to note that unlike  the mona lisa or my dollar bill nfts  aren't physical items instead their  digital code stored and secured on a  blockchain simply put the blockchain is  a digital form of recordkeeping think of  it like a bank statement or a cvs  receipt just a really long list of  transactions but this list isn't just  yours it's a part of a peer-to-peer  network meaning other people have access  to it as well and each new transaction  isn't bounced between banks in order to  be verified it's checked by every  computer that has a copy of that list  it's decentralized and its structure  essentially makes the timeline of data  slash transactions nearly impossible to  tamper with maintaining the blockchain  and facilitating all of the transactions  that take place requires incredible  amounts of processing power and  according to a report from bbc tech  bitcoin blockchain consumes more  electricity in a year than all of  argentina so needless to say it's  horrible for our environment nfts exist  on various blockchains but most being  sold are found on the ethereum  blockchain ethereum reportedly uses less  electricity but it's far from being  emission free  back on december 7 2021 ubisoft  announced its first nft project titled  ubisoft quartz however they're not the  first to merge gaming in nfts as a  matter of fact the first big use case  was actually a game called cryptokitties  this game launched in 2017 and allows  players to purchase nfts for specific  digital cats these cats can then breed  with other digital cat purchases and  produce entirely new cats that can then  be sold as unique nfts of their own this  market has generated millions since its  inception with some single cats costing  well over a million dollars alone axi  infinity is a turn-based combat game in  which users can raise battle and trade  fantasy creatures called axes i've heard  it called pokemon on the blockchain but  for some reason i don't like to think  about it that way the game is made by  vietnam-based sky mavis which was  founded in 2018 and is now valued at 3  billion  axiomfinity was reportedly inspired by  cryptokitties and extremely rare axes  can reportedly go for more than 1  million  another example is the sandbox a  community driven creative space that  lets users create voxel-based assets or  gaming experiences you could say it's  similar to roblox outside of the fact  that these gaming experience all exist  on finite virtual lan that users can buy  using nfts the tokens correlate to a  piece of land where you can host game  creations for other players to visit  with the value of the size and position  of the land determined by simple supply  and demand but on the other side of  things now we have the first aaa nft  project in ubisoft quartz courts will  wrap up in-game cosmetics into their own  nfts each of which will have a unique  serial number and history of owners  these items called digits can then be  freely traded between players for agreed  upon prices with a natural economy  forming around these transactions and  the scarcity of the items the program  which is still in beta is currently  supported by ghost recon breakpoint on  pc and only available in certain regions  despite using a decentralized system  called tezos this blockchain uses proof  of stake as opposed to the energy-hungry  proof of work blockchains such as  bitcoin and ethereum leading ubisoft to  call its venture quote energy efficient  but did ubisoft need to use blockchain  technology at all one of the supposed  advantages of blockchain is that it's  decentralized but in the case of quartz  digits are stuck in ubisoft's ecosystem  available in a handful of countries and  is only authorized to be traded on two  third-party platforms so to me quartz is  just the same old in-game cosmetic  system except now it's wearing a costume  and trying to fool you into doing things  a different way it's hard for me to  believe that these exclusive items  couldn't have been implemented without  blockchain as kyle orland of rs technica  puts it quote a basic centrally  controlled database could do the same  thing much more simply  according to ubisoft this is the first  step in what will be a four year venture  into blockchain technology and the  company's pursuit of its own metaverse  at the time of this recording digit  support has not been announced for any  other games but given ubisoft's large  library my guess is it's only a matter  of time before that changes that is of  course assuming that this first run  within break point is a successful one  the game's first 2 000 digits were  released on november 9th and quickly  sold out so there are clearly people  interested however when you weigh that  number against the over 20 000 that  dislike the announcement video you start  to see that the negative reception far  outweighs the positive but ubisoft  aren't the only publisher looking at  blockchain technologies and nfts in  particular companies like ea take 2 and  even square enix are enthusiastic about  the venture ea ceo andrew wilson even  went as far as to call nft and  blockchain games quote the future of our  industry during an earnings call a  statement that's not too far off from  ubisoft's ceo calling it a quote  revolution in the industry meanwhile  valve is taking steps to keep it away  from their platform back in october the  company revealed games that use  blockchain technology or let users  exchange nfts and cryptocurrencies will  not be allowed on steam steam's  competitor the epic game store does not  have such a policy in place and told the  verge it's open to the idea of games  that use nfts and cryptos so where are  we headed from here honestly it's  too hard to know for sure to investors  and crypto enthusiasts nfts and video  games might seem like a match made in  heaven however to the gaming community  who have been fighting against micro  transactions and loot boxes for years  this is just another way for publishers  to cash in on the next big thing and  that's what's worrisome for me we've  already seen certain non-free to play  games get stripped of proper progression  in order to push microtransactions so  how long until we see nft shift gaming  experiences in a similar way and what  happens when the other publishers join  in are they going to choose something  energy efficient as well or is the  environment going to take another hit  for some subjectively cool looking  helmet in a video game the harsh reality  is that we're definitely going to see  more companies take steps forward in  this space throughout 2022 and when i  say that i'm including them talking  about their plans even further as well  as actually launching court's  competitors however there is a massive  divide between the people who are for it  and those who are against it so it's  unclear just how successful any of those  ventures will become by 2023 similar to  fads like motion controls 3d gaming and  in some cases vr and battle royales  companies just see dollar signs and want  their piece of the pie so if this video  tells you anything make sure you hit the  like button because it's also now in nft  selling for a million dollars put the  money right here right here and it's all  yours this rare video all yours  [Music]
 

